{"abstention":null,"candidates":[{"claim":"Safe means the operation produces no state change on any invocation (read-only); idempotent means repeating the operation has the same effect as doing it once, but the first invocation may change state. RFC 9110 establishes safe ⊂ idempotent. An append-only event log requires idempotence of its writers: appending the same event twice must yield the same log as appending it once (via deduplication on event identity), but appending is inherently state-mutating so safety is neither sufficient nor desirable.","counterconditions":["If RFC 9110 does not explicitly state safe ⊂ idempotent, the hierarchy claim weakens to 'safe implies idempotent by construction'","If the event log's deduplication mechanism is external to the writer (e.g., log-side filtering), the writer itself need not be idempotent","If at-least-once delivery is replaced by exactly-once, idempotence becomes unnecessary"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"RFC 9110 §9.2.1 defines safe methods as read-only (no server state change), §9.2.2 defines idempotent methods as having identical effect when repeated. Safety is strictly stronger: all safe methods are idempotent, but PUT/DELETE are idempotent-not-safe. For the event log, at-least-once delivery causes duplicate appends. Idempotent writers detect duplicates (e.g., by event ID or content hash) and discard them, leaving log state identical to the single-delivery case. A 'safe' writer cannot append at all, so it cannot fulfill the log's purpose. The minimal sufficient property is idempotence.","optional_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_006","SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"claim":"The RFC 9110 safe/idempotent distinction is HTTP-method-semantics-local and does not transfer cleanly to event-log writer semantics. The append-only log does not require RFC-style idempotence or safety; it requires a domain-specific replay-determinism property (deduplication on deterministic event identity) that is analogous to idempotence but defined over the log's own state model, not HTTP response semantics.","counterconditions":["If RFC 9110's definition of idempotence is general enough to cover any state machine (not HTTP-specific), then the transfer is valid","If the log is exposed over HTTP and the writer uses HTTP methods, the distinction collapses"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"RFC 9110 defines safe and idempotent over HTTP request/response semantics (observable effects on server state and response equivalence). An append-only event log writer operates over a different state space (the log itself). The property it needs—'appending event E twice yields the same log as appending E once'—resembles idempotence but is defined over log content, not HTTP responses. Conflating the two risks category error: an HTTP-idempotent PUT could still corrupt a log if it lacks deduplication.","optional_refs":["SRC_007"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.35},{"claim":"The precise distinction is about which invocation matters: safety constrains the first invocation (it must be a no-op on state), while idempotence constrains the Nth invocation (it must match the first). The event log requires the Nth-invocation property because at-least-once delivery only causes problems on retries (N>1), not on the initial append. Therefore the log requires idempotence, not safety.","counterconditions":["If idempotence is defined as 'same effect on every invocation' rather than 'same effect as the first', the framing changes","If the log tolerates duplicate events (e.g., consumers deduplicate downstream), even idempotence is unnecessary"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Safety says: invocation #1 does not change state. Idempotence says: invocation #N has the same effect as invocation #1. For an event log, invocation #1 must change state (append), so safety is violated by design. Invocation #2+ (retries from at-least-once delivery) must not create duplicates, which is the idempotence property. The two properties constrain different invocations, and the log's failure mode is exclusively in the retry (N>1) case.","optional_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"claim":"Counterexample to 'the log requires safety': a safe writer, by definition, cannot append events (appending changes state). If the log required safe writers, no events would ever be written. Therefore safety is not just insufficient—it is impossible for the log's function. The log requires idempotence: the only property that permits state change on first write while protecting against duplicate writes from retries.","counterconditions":["If 'safe' is reinterpreted outside RFC 9110 to mean 'safe to retry' (conflating with idempotence), the counterexample fails","If the log's state model treats appends as read-only metadata indexing (implausible), safety could hold"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Assume the log requires safe writers. By RFC 9110 §9.2.1, a safe method produces no change to server state. Appending an event changes the log state. Therefore a safe writer cannot append. The log receives zero events and fails its purpose. This counterexample eliminates safety as a candidate requirement. Idempotence (§9.2.2) permits state change on first invocation while guaranteeing repeated invocations are harmless, which is exactly the at-least-once delivery resilience needed.","optional_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_006","SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"claim":"The event log actually requires neither idempotence nor safety as defined by RFC 9110 if the deduplication burden is placed on the log infrastructure rather than on the writers. In an architecture where the log itself assigns sequence numbers and rejects duplicates by event hash, individual writers need not be idempotent; only the log's ingestion layer must be.","counterconditions":["If the problem explicitly attributes the requirement to writers (it says 'of its writers'), then the system-level argument is out of scope","If the writer controls event identity assignment and the log blindly accepts, deduplication falls back on the writer","If RFC 9110's notion of idempotence is method-level and the log's append API is the method, then the log API itself must be idempotent regardless of individual writer behavior"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"RFC 9110's properties are attributed to the method (the writer's operation). But in event-sourcing systems, deduplication is often infrastructure-side: the log's append API checks for a client-supplied event ID and silently drops duplicates. The writer's operation is not itself idempotent (it blindly appends), but the system as a whole achieves replay safety. The problem's phrasing 'requires of its writers' may be a false presupposition: the requirement is on the system, not the writer.","optional_refs":["SRC_007"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.2},{"claim":"Both safe and idempotent are defined in RFC 9110 as properties of HTTP methods, but the critical operational distinction for replay is that idempotence tolerates state change on first invocation while safety forbids it entirely. The append-only log requires idempotence precisely because it needs first-invocation mutation (append) plus retry-safety (no duplicate). This is the conjunction 'mutable-once, stable-on-repeat,' which is exactly idempotence-minus-safety.","counterconditions":["If idempotence in RFC 9110 is defined only over response equivalence and not state equivalence, then (2) may not hold for log deduplication","If the event log's append is modeled as PATCH (non-idempotent), the analogy to PUT fails"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Define the needed property P as: (1) first invocation changes state (append succeeds), (2) Nth invocation (N>1) leaves state as after invocation 1 (no duplicate). Safety gives (2) but contradicts (1). Idempotence gives (2) and is compatible with (1). The needed property is therefore idempotence that is not safety—i.e., the idempotent-not-safe methods like PUT. The log writer is analogous to PUT: it writes (mutates) but is safe to retry.","optional_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_006"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7}],"context_request":null,"research_proposals":[{"purpose":"Fetch RFC 9110 §9.2 to obtain verbatim definitions of 'safe' and 'idempotent' methods, confirm whether the RFC explicitly states safe ⊂ idempotent, and determine whether idempotence is defined over server state, response equivalence, or both.","request_identifier":"rfc9110_sec9_2","urls":["https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-9.2"]}],"scratch_proposal":{"links":[{"from_ref":"NEW_001","holds_when":"RFC 9110 §9.2 fetched and confirms safe ⊆ idempotent","relation_hint":"NEW_001's subset hierarchy claim depends on SCR_002's resolution","to_ref":"SCR_002"}],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Working synthesis: The distinction is that safe forbids state change on any invocation (read-only), while idempotent permits state change on first invocation but guarantees repeated invocations have the same effect. RFC 9110 establishes safe ⊆ idempotent. The event log requires idempotent writers because (1) appending mutates state (not safe), (2) retries from at-least-once delivery must not duplicate. Pending: verbatim RFC 9110 §9.2 confirmation of the subset relationship and whether idempotence is defined over state or response.","unfinished":"Resolve SCR_002/SCR_003 with fetched RFC text.","why_keep_this":"Synthesizes the core answer pending research-fetch confirmation of RFC 9110 text."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Does RFC 9110 define idempotence over server state equivalence, response equivalence, or both? This determines whether the event-log deduplication requirement is literally the same property as HTTP idempotence or merely analogous.","related_refs":["SCR_003","NEW_001","SRC_007"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}